00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.790 align:start position:0%
 
International Rescue Committee has  warned that up to a billion people could  be infected with coronavirus across  crisis affected countries I spoke  earlier to its chief executive the  former Foreign Secretary David Miliband  you told me that the virus could claim  as many as 3 million lives around the  world we've studied the 34 fragile and  conflict affected states where the  International Rescue Committee works we  know the struggles that exist in  advanced countries like the UK or the US  but in these conflict torn States using  very conservative estimates on the  infection rate based on the Imperial  College and World Health Organization  data based on a assumption about Chinese  levels of hospital and health care which  is really very generous given the levels  that exist in conflict-affected States  like Yemen or Syria we've found that the  corona virus is going to affect between  half a billion and a billion people  globally in these 34 countries and that  the death toll could number up to 3  million between 1 and a half and 3  million and so our call is to say that  we have only weeks to prepare for this  disease to go on the rampage in some of  the poorest countries in the world and  as the debate about lockdown develops  and how to end it develops in countries  like the UK we need to make sure that we  recognize this disease is a global  disease that will only be fully beaten  when it's beaten everywhere now this at  the moment it's a dreadful prospect but  it's only potential what is the extent  of coronavirus infection as far as we  can tell in in these 34 countries well  we know that corona virus is now  everywhere we also know that the testing  regime for all the problems that exist  in the UK or the u.s. the testing regime  is threadbare in countries like South  Sudan in countries like Nigeria in  countries like Pakistan and so the  official toll at the moment which in in  Africa they're estimated according to  the number of tests is about only 25,000  cases across the whole continent about  more than a billion people we know  that's a massive underestimate what we  can see is that step by step there are  more and more people coming to our  health centers that we run around the  world and so we see this disease really  taking off by around mid-may and so we  can see it creeping forward we've got a  few weeks left to do some essential  cautionary preventative work that means  very basic hand-washing stations  it means triaging people by testing  their fever and isolating a high fever  cases but we're going to also have to  build up the health systems because  there's going to be an enormous number  of people who need basic therapeutic  care and where are the resources for  that going to come from well the  resources are gonna have to come  internationally one thing that we've  discovered in the last a few weeks is  that obviously countries like the UK the  u.s. they found money when they needed  it and our point is that as a matter of  morality but also as a hard-headed  matter about how we return to anything  like normality we're going to have to  fully fund the aspirations of  organizations like the World Health  Organization the promise of the Trump  administration to cut the World Health  Organization budget is the opposite of  what's needed we also are going to have  to make sure that in countries like  South Sudan where there's four  ventilators for the whole country we're  not gonna save lives by getting  ventilators in there we're gonna save  lives by getting much more basic  infrastructure into place so that people  can prevent the disease through hand  washing through proper information  through a modicum of social distancing  but also by isolating cases so they  don't spread across the whole population  so if the World Health Organization were  to be funded fully and most of its  members do fund it would that be  sufficient well the new appeal details  haven't yet come out we're gonna know  next week the global humanitarian  response appeal which is jointly being  done by different agencies across the  United Nations so we don't yet know the  full scale of their appeal but certainly  the underfunding that is promised by the  Trump administration is completely  retrograde it would damage some  absolutely vital programs not just in  respect of Cova incidentally but also in  respect of other matters 27%  u.s. funding for the World Health  Organization goes into eradicating polio  so there are other health conditions and  we've made the point that there's a  double emergency in developed in under  the in lesser developed countries in  countries where there's a very weak  health infrastructure yes there's a  health emergency but there's also a  social and economic emergency looming as  well where the weakness of the overall  economy means that the emergency is  doubled for these very vulnerable  populations and so the sooner we get on  with the preventative work the more we  can mitigate both aspects of the  emergency what do you say to President  Trump's suggestion that in warmer places  the virus may pose less of a threat we  don't have any evidence for that at the  moment there are warm places that have  had the disease remember the in  Australia it's been summer over the last  three months and they've had the disease  so I don't think we can rely on the  weather to sort this disease out it's  hit countries warm and cold it's hit  countries democratic and autocratic it's  hit countries north south east and west  it's a genuine disease of the connected  global economy and society and will only  attack and push this disease back if we  do it everywhere the biggest part of the  problem there's also been a suggestion  in countries where sanitation is less  good the population may have a bigger in  bill resistance to things like this  that's certainly the case in on one side  of the ledger but on the other side of  the ledger countries where there's high  levels of malnutrition where there are  weak underlying health conditions are  more vulnerable so it swings and  roundabouts on this that's why we've  given a range of a half a billion to a  billion estimates of infection in our  report we've also given the range for  the number who will eventually die  between one and a half and three million  because it depends on our response and  of course there's a lot that's uncertain  about this disease but what we do know  is that if you can't wash your hands if  you've got high levels of malnutrition  if you don't have a functioning health  system your  vulnerability is all the greater on the  other side of the ledger of course if  you've got a youthful population that  may help you but the vulnerability the  danger for people around the world  remains very high now on this question  you obviously I tried to provide some  leadership but if we look at national  governments you're critical of Donald  Trump but do you see a sufficient global  leadership coming from elsewhere not at  the moment no I mean it's to state the  obvious there is that this is a global  disease that's being fought nation by  nation now the UK has taken an important  step in convening a group of countries  to help think about the distribution of  vaccines when one is finally discovered  that's a good thing but we need much  much more of that at the moment the  group of seven leading industrialized  democracies haven't even managed to  agree a joint statement the group of 20  which is currently led by Saudi Arabia  which is 20 largest economies in the  world that is in a slumber that's the  only way to describe it and the global  nature of the disease is not being  matched by the kind of international  cooperation that is going to be  essential we can also see a looming  battle where you can see countries like  China or to classic countries or Thorat  Aryan countries saying they've dealt  with it better because they're  authoritarian there's no evidence that  that's the case because after all  democracies like Germany and South Korea  have done well but I do think that these  contests about the lessons of the  disease are being started now and the  debates about the lessons of disease are  being started now they need to be joined  by real action do you see China also  trying to exploit perhaps an  illegitimate opportunity for global  influence in this area undoubtedly that  every country wants that's thinking  strategically autocratic one yes I think  that you can see both in the benevolent  actions that China is taking for example  in the distribution of humanitarian aid  but also the tougher line they're taking  in their own sphere of influence in in  the region and in their own country or  the way they're cracking down on dissent  you can see there's a two-step being  a practiced and my point is that we  shouldn't be declaring a cold war  against China that would be a retrograde  and thing to do it wouldn't be a wise  thing to do but we do need to rally the  world's democracies to work together so  that they can negotiate compete and  cooperate with countries like China on  something like an equal basis because  given the size of China you're right it  isn't emerging superpower but if you  balance that with the scale of the  European Union 500 million people plus  the UK plus the US population you've got  some kind of balance there and I think  if the you if the democracy of the world  don't cooperate they're gonna be on the  backfoot when it comes to a more  confident China which we're undoubtedly  seeing but what does it say that the  United States which has been the leader  of the free world for at least a century  the biggest economy in the world the  most advanced society what does it say  for democracy the United States is also  the worst-hit country but it says that  there is a high degree of dysfunction in  the way the American response has been  organized some of that is built into the  separation of powers between national  and federal and state authorities but  obviously there's also the degree of  polarization that has created the  massive level of mistrust that exists  across the country here I'm living and  working in the u.s. I'm obviously a  British citizen and I see a high level  of polarization and mistrust and one of  the things that we know about this  crisis is that it requires high levels  of social trust for an effective  response countries like South Korea and  Germany have shown that the rallying  that's happened in the UK to support the  National Health Service 750,000  volunteers that speaks to social  cohesion we're not seeing that in the  u.s. at the moment even before you get  into the absurdities of last week's  discussion about bleach and it's ill  effects and so I think that there's a  big question now that America is going  to have to confront how does it combine  a more effective domestic response with  a reassertion of international  leadership and in an election year that  needs to be a big part of the debate

